Most DevOps Engineers Get Rejected After This ONE Question
“Tell me about a real production issue you handled end-to-end.”
Not:
 ❌ “What is Kubernetes?”
 ❌ “Explain CI/CD”
👉 This one decides your interview.
🧠 What Interviewers Are REALLY Testing
They don’t care if you can recite docs.
They’re checking:
 ✔️ Ownership
 ✔️ Debugging mindset 🔍
 ✔️ Decision-making under pressure
 ✔️ Real production experience

❌ Why Most Candidates Fail
They say:
“I checked logs… restarted the pod… it worked.”
Congrats. You pressed the restart button. Very heroic.
But this shows:
 🚫 No root cause
 🚫 No thinking
 🚫 No prevention
 ⚙️ What a Strong Answer Looks Like

Structure your answer like this:
🔍 1. Detection
# Check logs
kubectl logs <pod-name>

# Check pod events
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

👉 Alerts? Monitoring? What triggered you?

📊 2. Investigation
# Check resource usage
kubectl top pod

# Check rollout status
kubectl rollout status deployment <name>

👉 CPU normal? Memory spiking? Network issue?

🧪 3. Hypothesis & Validation
👉 What did you suspect first?
👉 What did you rule out (and why)?
This is where most people go blank.

🔥 4. Root Cause
Not:
 “I restarted it”
But:
 👉 Config issue?
 👉 Memory leak?
 👉 Bad deployment?

🛠️ 5. Fix (Short-Term + Long-Term)
# Rollback deployment
kubectl rollout undo deployment <name>

✔️ Immediate fix
✔️ Permanent solution
🧠 6. Prevention
👉 Added alerts 📊
👉 Updated configs
👉 Improved deployment checks
👉 Better monitoring
💡 Real Answer (What Impresses)

Instead of:
 “I restarted the service”
Say:
 👉 “We saw a spike in 5xx errors from alerts. CPU was stable but memory kept increasing. Logs didn’t show errors, so we checked metrics. Found a memory leak after a config change. Rolled back, fixed limits, added alerts, and updated deployment checklist.”
That’s not theory.
That’s production thinking.
🎯 One Truth

Anyone can run:
kubectl get pods

Very few can explain:
 👉 Why the pod failed
 👉 What they learned
 👉 How they prevented it
 🔥 Closing Line
If you can clearly explain one real incident end-to-end,
you’re already ahead of most candidates.

